A roll-up lives or dies on repeatability. One-off deals reward instinct; a platform strategy rewards a process that turns a wide funnel into signed LOIs at a predictable rate, month after month. The teams that compound do not chase every target that lands in the inbox. They run a pipeline the way an operator runs a factory: defined stages, clear entry and exit criteria, and honest conversion math.

## Start with a thesis narrow enough to disqualify

Before sourcing a single target, write down what you are buying and why. A usable thesis names the sub-sector, the size band, the geography, the margin profile, and the reason ownership under your platform is worth more than ownership alone. The test of a good thesis is not how many targets it includes; it is how many it lets you reject in the first five minutes.

Concretely, define:

- Revenue and EBITDA range you will consider, and the hard floor below which you pass
- Ownership and succession signals that make a seller ready, such as age, absentee ownership, or no clear successor
- Customer concentration, contract length, and recurring revenue thresholds
- The integration angle: shared back office, cross-selling, procurement leverage, or geographic density

## Define the stages and the gates between them

Most pipelines fail not because deals stall, but because no one agrees on what stage a deal is actually in. Fix that with named stages and a single exit criterion for each. A workable default:

1. **Sourced.** A target that fits the thesis on the surface. Exit: basic financials requested.
2. **Qualified.** Fit confirmed against the thesis and a seller willing to talk. Exit: a preliminary valuation range.
3. **Engaged.** Management call held, high-level numbers reviewed. Exit: indication of interest submitted.
4. **Under LOI.** Terms agreed in principle and exclusivity granted. Exit: diligence kickoff.
5. **In diligence.** Confirmatory work underway. Exit: signed purchase agreement.

The discipline is in the gates. A deal does not move to Engaged because you like it; it moves because the exit criterion for Qualified is met. Written gates end the weekly debate about which deals are real.

## Score every target the same way

Gut feel does not scale across a team reviewing dozens of targets a month. A simple scorecard does. Rate each target on a handful of weighted factors tied directly to the thesis: strategic fit, financial quality, integration effort, seller readiness, and price expectation. Keep the scale short, three or five points, and keep the factors stable so scores are comparable across the whole funnel.

The score is not a verdict; it is a triage tool. It tells you where to spend the scarcest resource on any deal team, which is attention. High-fit, high-readiness targets get a call this week. Everything else gets a nurture cadence.

## Build the funnel math before you need it

A roll-up is a numbers game with a long lag. If you want to close one platform and three add-ons this year, work backward through your own conversion rates. If one in five qualified targets reaches LOI and one in two LOIs closes, then four closings require roughly forty qualified targets, which requires a far wider top of funnel. Most teams discover their sourcing is understaffed only after two quiet quarters. Model it first.

Track conversion between each stage, not just the count at each stage. Stage-to-stage conversion is where the truth lives. A fat top of funnel with thin Qualified-to-Engaged conversion means your sourcing filters are wrong, not your closers.

## Run a cadence that keeps deals warm

Proprietary deal flow is a relationship business, and relationships decay without contact. Set a standing cadence: a weekly pipeline review that walks the board by stage and forces a next action and a date on every live deal, and a nurture rhythm for targets that are a good fit but not yet ready. Many of the best add-ons close eighteen months after first contact, and they close with the buyer who stayed in touch without being a nuisance.

Assign a single owner to every deal. Shared ownership is no ownership. The owner is accountable for the next action, the date, and the honest stage.

## What good looks like

A healthy roll-up pipeline is boring in the best way. The board reflects reality because the gates are written down. Conversion rates are known, so sourcing targets are set with intent rather than hope. No deal sits for three weeks without a next action. New analysts can read the board and understand every deal without a translator, because the stages and scores mean the same thing to everyone.

That consistency is the whole point. It lets you compare this quarter to last, spot a sourcing shortfall early, and defend your capital allocation with data instead of anecdotes.

Open Deal Book is built to hold this operating system in one place: a pipeline with stages and gates you define, scoring on your own criteria, and conversion analytics that read straight off the board, so the process lives in the platform rather than in a spreadsheet only one person understands. It is self-hostable, which matters when the pipeline is your strategy and you would rather not hand it to a third party. The discipline is yours to build; the system is here to keep it honest.
